Tokens:
    The smallest mathematical units (like words or parts of words) that an LLM breaks text down into so a computer can process it.

Vectors & Embeddings:
    Multi-dimensional numbers that map words into a "meaning space," allowing the AI to understand context and relationships between words.

Temperature:
    A settings parameter that controls how creative, random, or strictly predictable the AI's next-word choices will be.

RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback):
    A fine-tuning method where humans rank and score AI responses to teach the model what answers are safe and helpful.

Hallucination:
    A design flaw where an AI confidently presents completely made-up facts or code as absolute truth because it doesn't know its own knowledge boundaries.

Agent:
    An LLM running autonomously in a continuous loop that can use external tools, check its own work, and retry until a task is completed.

Skills:
    Markdown files (specifically used in tools like Claude Code) that teach an AI domain-specific knowledge or conventions only when they are actively needed.

MCP (Model Context Protocol):
    A standardized "API" that allows AI models to directly and uniformly communicate with external tools like Slack, Notion, or databases.

RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation):
    A system architecture that creates a boundary around an AI, forcing it to look up answers only within your specific internal company documents.

Context Window:
    The maximum amount of total data (including chat history and background prompts) that an AI can process at one single time before running out of "memory."

